    "result_text": "The build is green. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the \"reject a future `registrationDate` with 400\" rule following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, reusing the existing validation/escalation pattern rather than adding logic to an existing class.\n\n**New files:**\n- `ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate.java` \u2014 a small single-responsibility function that reads the supplied `registrationDate` from the shared request; skips a null date (leaving it for `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate`) and throws `FutureRegistrationDateException` when it is after `LocalDate.now()`. It mutates nothing.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateException.java` \u2014 a checked exception (extends `Exception`), mirroring `InvalidPostcodeException`.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds 400 with an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to the handler.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted a `validateRegistrationDate` step into `owners.POST.yml` between `normalizeEmail` and `defaultRegistrationDate`, so the supplied value is validated *before* it is defaulted or rolled to a business day.\n\nThe full test suite passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
